<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sámi people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sami, Saami</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sámi are the indigenous people of the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Kola Peninsula, a region they call Sápmi. Traditionally they lived by fishing, hunting, trapping and later reindeer herding, and they speak several related Uralic languages. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> they paid tribute and taxes to Norse and later Scandinavian and Russian rulers, and they now have their own parliaments in Norway, Sweden and Finland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sami-people.jpg" alt="Sámi people" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeltz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sami_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sámi are the traditionally Sámi-speaking indigenous people of Sápmi, formerly called Lapland. In English they were long known as Lapps, a term some Sámi regard as offensive. Their languages form a branch of the Uralic family. The groups that reached the Finnish Lakeland between 1600 and 1500 BCE later became the Sámi, who arrived in Sápmi during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> or early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</p><p>The first probable mention is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, about 98 CE, who called them Fenni. Norse speakers used Finn for the Sámi, as in the Icelandic Eddas and sagas of the eleventh to fourteenth centuries. The word Lapp appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a>'s twelfth-century Gesta Danorum, and Johannes Schefferus's Lapponia of 1673 made it standard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For centuries the Sámi and the Scandinavians had little contact. The Sámi lived mainly in the inland north, while Scandinavians gradually settled the Norwegian coast. Until the bubonic plague reached northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1349 the two peoples occupied separate economic niches, the Sámi herding reindeer or fishing along the fjords. How far south the Sámi settled has long been debated: Yngvar Nielsen, commissioned by the Norwegian government in 1889, concluded they had lived no farther south than Lierne until about 1500, but later archaeological finds suggest a presence in southern Norway in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p>From the eighteenth and especially the nineteenth century the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> asserted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> more aggressively in the north and pursued forced assimilation. Generations of Sámi children were taken to missionary or state-run boarding schools, and laws denied the Sámi rights to their beliefs, language, land and livelihoods. In the 1970s the idea of Sápmi as a Sámi nation gained acceptance, and a flag, an anthem and a national day followed in the 1980s and 1990s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditional livelihoods include coastal fishing, fur trapping and sheep herding, and the best known is semi-nomadic reindeer herding. Herders traditionally lived in groups of several families called siidat. About 10 percent of Sámi are connected to herding, which in some regions of the Nordic countries is legally reserved for them.</p><p>There are ten distinct Sámi languages, closely related but not mutually intelligible. Many Sámi kept their ancestral religion until the eighteenth century, and most now belong to the Lutheran churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. The joik, a song-chant sung a cappella, is characteristic of the music, and the craft duodji makes useful objects from wood, bone, antler and leather. The Laponian Area in Swedish Lappland became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sámi people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Finnish and Swedish were both official languages, and the Swedish-speaking minority, once the country’s elite, kept its own schools and institutions; the <b>Sámi</b> of the north remained a small indigenous minority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The singer Cem Adrian put out a live recording from 2010 in 2017, and in 2014 the British-Azerbaijani singer <b>Sami</b> Yusuf recorded a version mainly in English that ends in Azerbaijani.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sari-gelin">Sari Gelin</a></li><li><blockquote>Recorded versions include those of Oum Kalthoum, Mishary Rashid Alafasy, <b>Sami</b> Yusuf, Yusuf Islam (Cat Stevens), Mesut Kurtis, Native Deen, Raef, Maher Zain, Junaid Jamshed, Payam Azizi and Marufur Rahman.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tala-al-badru-alayna">Tala al-Badru Alayna</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hålogaland lords controlled trade in furs, walrus ivory, hides and dried fish, much of it taken as tribute from the <b>Sámi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/halogaland">Hålogaland</a></li></ul></section>
